  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and reading the rule back showed it enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" covers `kibana_sample_data_logs`, and its time field is `timestamp`. It has about 45 fields. The ones I used are `response` (text) and `response.keyword` (keyword, aggregatable).\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-0cfbc673 Web logs\" (id `df4f2679-6605-4617-be67-3578d3e6c7b4`) has two panels. \"Requests over time\" is a line chart of the request count. The other is a pie chart of the request count by `response.keyword`, showing up to 20 codes.\n3. **Rule:** \"S4-0cfbc673 5xx errors\" (id `02576ba7-1fdb-454d-af60-248ac558f18b`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It checks every minute and fires when more than 10 documents match in the last 5 minutes. It matches `response.keyword` from \"500\" to \"599\" and has no actions attached. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it.\n4. **Panel edit:** I changed the response-code panel's title to \"Responses by code\". The dashboard now lists \"Requests over time\" and \"Responses by code\".\n\nThe 500\u2013599 match compares strings, not numbers, because `response` is a text field. That is exact for three-digit codes.\n\nI haven't checked that the rule actually fires on the sample data.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
